i'm here with frances haugen she is a  former facebook employee a whistleblower  who put forward a complaint against the  company with u.s law enforcement and  testified before the u.s congress when  she left the company she brought with  her a trove of documents about private  facebook research she said that these  documents show that the company is aware  that its platforms amplify  misinformation hate and political unrest  that the company isn't doing enough to  change this and is hiding what it knows  ms haugen thank you for being with us  today um you  have said numerous times that uh these  documents point to facebook not doing  anything about its platforms promoting  um misinformation and hate could you  give us some examples of how this work  and what facebook isn't doing  i want to be clear it's not that  facebook isn't doing anything facebook  has pointed out they've invested  something like 16 billion i think that's  13 billion dollars since since 2016  on issues around safety and integrity  but the question is how much should they  be spending  is that is the amount of spending that  they have today enough and facebook's  documents are point to over and over  again that there are many many solutions  many of them not even content-based that  would make the platform safer for  everyone one of the core things that i'm  i'm trying to draw attention to is the  underinvestment in languages that aren't  english  they know that they face most regulatory  scrutiny in the united states and so  they try the hardest to keep the  platform nice in the u.s  unfortunately the most fragile places in  the world are also the most diverse when  it comes to languages  facebook's current strategy for safety  is that mark has said mark zuckerberg  has said engagement based ranking is  dangerous because people are drawn to  extreme content  but don't worry  ai will save us  the problem is that strategy requires us  to build those ai systems over and over  and over again in each language for the  platform to be safe and right now  facebook is not doing that  and um  so is it just a question of investment  and putting more money in there should  facebook change its algorithms what  should the company itself do  i'm a big proponent that you know if we  rolled back the clock and looked at what  facebook was like in 2008 2009 like  before we had an ai telling us what to  focus on we had a facebook that was  about our family and friends  facebook has said  we can't do time-based ranking you won't  like it yeah if you took the current  facebook today and switched it over to a  world where the ai wasn't focusing our  attention it's true you wouldn't like it  but if we redesign the platforms with  the idea that they should be human scale  not  mega scale with an ai focusing us around  those would be platforms that were about  our family and friends we could enjoy  them and they would be a lot safer  and you're now  in europe you're speaking to politicians  governments you're going to be speaking  at the european parliament  when it comes to reining in facebook  meta  and big tech  what do you expect from europe  i'm hoping that europe will stand up for  consumers because right now consumers  don't know if what's in the can is  what's on the label and what we see from  the disclosures that i've made is that  facebook is misconstruing what their  product is  the european union has an opportunity to  enforce transparency on the platforms in  a privacy sensitive way  and they have the ability to have  regulatory muscle to make facebook  disclose what our challenges it believes  exist with the platforms right now  people can't make informed choices on  whether or not to use these things and i  feel like that is that degrades our  autonomy because we don't have the  information to make good choices and  that violates our dignity  and um do you think the plans that for  example the european union is currently  working on do they go far enough  i'm a big proponent of what i call the  one two three plan so right now uh  facebook would have to articulate a risk  assessment  facebook is not very diverse  you know they largely centered in menlo  park california eight hours from europe  and eight hours from asia just to make  it hard to talk to them  i think there should have to be a second  step which is i'm a big proponent there  should be a singular a single regulator  for europe  and that regulator's job would not be so  much to tell facebook what to do but  would be to make sure that consumers had  the information they needed to make the  decisions so you can imagine that  regulator going out to civil society  groups and saying  what are the problems that you're seeing  so the combination of what facebook  knows and what those groups know can be  a shared list of challenges  and then the third part of the one two  three is facebook should actually have  to articulate what are they going to do  to solve each of those problems  because right now facebook just waves  their hands at us and says we're working  on it  and if facebook had to articulate those  harms in tandem with the community and  had to articulate what to solve how they  were going to solve them people would be  able to make informed choices on whether  or not to use these platforms and we've  seen facebook meta announced that it was  working on this metaverse an  all-encompassing future version of the  internet um how much does that worry you  i think the announcement around facebook  pivoting to video games illustrates a  meta problem for facebook which is that  over and over again facebook prioritizes  growth and expansion over making sure  that they've actually done a good job  with what they already have  i was shocked and frankly outraged when  facebook said that they could afford 10  000 engineers to build video games  because if we had had 10 000 engineers  to work on safety  we would have a profoundly different  platform one that i think all of us  would enjoy more  and do you think that this uh change of  name especially meta is something that  signifies a change within the company  and a pivot within the company  or is it a move that aims to clean up  maybe the company's image  i think it's a classic crisis playbook  that when you don't like the narrative  you change the narrative  i think facebook decided to rush out  this launch because they were hoping it  would distract us from the harms that i  articulated in my disclosure we need to  hold facebook accountable and say we  don't want you to put your sensors in  our homes we don't want you to spy on us  we don't want to have to expose  ourselves further until we trust that  you're going to do a good job because  you have consistently demonstrated that  you will lie to the public if it's in  your own best interests  how much responsibility does facebook  ceo mark zuckerberg have when we look at  um what you revealed and what we've been  seeing come out about facebook's  handling of the situation i really don't  like to criticize individuals because i  really think that this is a systemic  problem this is about incentives and  about structure uh structures leading to  bad consequences but in the end face  mark zuckerberg has 54 of all the voting  shares for facebook he is probably one  of the most powerful unilateral leaders  in the world  at the end of the day he is responsible  for the system he built and he has the  ability to change that system and so  given that he would rather shift the  conversation to video games when people  are dying as a result of the actions of  facebook i think that means that mark  mark needs to  either say no i need a reset we need to  change  or he needs to let someone else do that  we can say that in many places around  the world facebook is almost the  internet it's true how much of a danger  is is that  one of the things that i think has  should have us hold facebook to a higher  standard is that facebook bought the  privilege of being the internet for the  majority of languages in the world it  subsidized people's usage of the  platform of its own platform and said  hey you can use anything you want but  you have to pay for that or you can use  our platform for free as a result for  the majority of languages in the world  80 or 90 of all the content that exists  on the internet in those languages is on  facebook  i am deeply concerned about this because  as i said at the beginning the most  fragile places in the world are the most  linguistically diverse  and facebook has not demonstrated that  they are are willing to do the level of  care  and responsibility for those people that  is necessary  why was it so important for you to come  forward on this  i saw a pattern of behavior where i  believed  there was no chance the face would be  able to solve these problems in  isolation that there were too many  organizational pressures the incentives  were were misaligned  and i knew that if i didn't do something  and i saw what i feared was going to  happen continue to unfurl and like  ethiopia announced a state of crisis  yesterday because the situation there is  decayed so much  um  i knew i could never live with myself if  i watched you know 10 million 20 million  people over the next 20 years die  because of violence that was facilitated  by social media and so people often ask  me am i stressed right now like you're  talking to all these people like it's  such a whirlwind and compared to living  with that secret  like everything feels very calm right  now because i know  this is not a my problem anymore it's a  thing that we together can solve  you mentioned ethiopia you also tweeted  about it what role does social media and  facebook play in the situation  developing there right now  uh facebook has been tracking the  situation in ethiopia since q1 of 2020  right people have been talking about how  the escalating rhetoric on the on on  facebook around the dehumanization of  minorities in ethiopia has been a major  factor in the willingness to starve  certain parts of the population commit  violence against them  and this is one of these things where we  see over and over again  something that happens before ethnic  conflicts is usually the dehumanization  of people describing people like insects  when you have a platform that prioritize  prioritizes for distribution the most  incendiary content  you know even if a small minority is  describing some a group of people as  bugs  that that rhetoric is so much more  inflammatory and the platform  you know distributes it more um i worry  that ethiopia is just the opening  chapter in a novel that is too horrific  for us to want to read the end  and i think we all have an opportunity  especially here in europe to speak up  for those who don't have influence or  don't have a voice because europe is  strong enough to actually make a  difference  do you think users have already or are  developing awareness as to what  might be wrong with facebook what  happens with facebook and how important  is it for users to know  one of the reasons why i've been pushing  for this idea of you know we need risk  assessments that don't just involve a  company they also involve the community  is that people have been looking at  what's happening on facebook in  isolation they see maybe their own  experience of it  but they don't see the systemic problems  i think my disclosure has opened a crack  into  what the reality is on the on on this  platform  and i have been incredibly heartened by  the growing attention and the growing  awareness that we need to act  francis howard thank you very much  my pleasure anything to help

